* E1 automated (2026-10-05) -- and the /isolate premise corrected

CORRECTION to §7.2 / §8: alfe does NOT pass `/isolate`.
build-launch-argv (autolisp-front-end/source/backend-autocad.lisp) launches
`accoreconsole /i <dwg> /s <run.scr>` and the git history has no `/isolate`
ever. The LISPSYS 0 every batch probe reports therefore comes from the
runner's AutoCAD PROFILE (the registry), not from a pinning option -- and a
level written into the profile before launch is what a run sees.

E1 is now a job: encoding:experiment:lispsys-bom:autocad:windows (manual;
automatic when its files change -- probe family `lispsys-bom`), driving
scripts/run-lispsys-bom-probe.ps1 + autolisp-front-end/tests/scenarios/
entities/lispsys-bom-probe.lsp:
  - writes three fixtures, (setq *e1-value* "AéZ") with é as E9 (cp1252),
    C3 A9 (UTF-8), EF BB BF + C3 A9 (UTF-8 with BOM);
  - for LISPSYS 0, 1, 2: writes the level (and SECURELOAD 0, so the
    fixture folder needs no trust) into EVERY AutoCAD profile's Variables
    key, starts a fresh alfe / accoreconsole, has AutoCAD's NATIVE load read
    each fixture -- through (read "load"), since alfe rewrites a literal
    (load ...) into its own deporting loader -- and prints (strlen codes);
  - restores LISPSYS / SECURELOAD in every profile afterwards (finally).
Report: dist/encoding/lispsys-bom-autocad-Windows.txt. Results go here when
it has run.

** E1 results (2026-10-05, job 16931781178, AutoCAD 2022 = ACADVER 24.1)

LISPSYS was written into both profiles (<<Profil sans nom>>, Epure; both
held "0", String) and each run reported the level it was given, so the
profile write is how a level is set (no /isolate involved). AutoCAD's
native load, (strlen (vl-string->list *e1-value*)) of "AéZ":

| source file        | LISPSYS 0              | LISPSYS 1          | LISPSYS 2          |
|--------------------+------------------------+--------------------+--------------------|
| cp1252 (E9)        | (3 (65 233 90))        | (3 (65 233 90))    | (3 (65 233 90))    |
| UTF-8 (C3 A9)      | (4 (65 195 169 90))    | (3 (65 233 90))    | (3 (65 233 90))    |
| UTF-8 + BOM        | (4 (65 195 169 90))    | (3 (65 233 90))    | (3 (65 233 90))    |

So, measured (it was pjb's statement before):
  - LISPSYS 0: windows-1252, and the BOM is IGNORED (skipped, no error, no
    effect: the UTF-8 bytes still read as two characters);
  - LISPSYS 1 / 2: UTF-8, BOM or not -- and a file that is NOT valid UTF-8
    (the lone E9) is read as windows-1252 instead: a FALLBACK, not an error.
Profiles restored to "0" afterwards (report's last line).

* E3 results (2026-10-05, MR !416) -- the batch drain is the `file' situation

run-encoding-experiment.ps1 now has the CAD write (chr 233), (chr 128) and
(chr 8364) with the runtime's own drain call -- (open path "a") +
write-line, which is how princ reaches protocol/stdout.txt in batch mode --
and dumps the file's bytes:

| target (batch)                          | (chr 233) | (chr 128) | (chr 8364) | line end |
|-----------------------------------------+-----------+-----------+------------+----------|
| BricsCAD V25 Windows -B (job 16936990755) | E9        | 80        | 80         | CR LF    |
| AutoCAD 2022 accoreconsole (job 16936990756) | E9     | 80        | AC         | CR LF    |

So:
  - BricsCAD/Windows batch: windows-1252 -- U+20AC is written as its
    cp1252 byte 0x80, as the GUI console does (section 7.2). There is no
    separate "-B batch console" device: what alfe drains is the `file'
    situation, already measured as ANSI_1252 (section 7.1). E3 CLOSED.
  - AutoCAD/accoreconsole: same bytes for E9 / 80, but U+20AC keeps only
    its low byte (0xAC) -- the truncation its console output shows (7.2).
  - Both write CR LF.
Consequence for the defaults table: `cadstdio' on both Windows CADs is
windows-1252 + CR LF, product-fixed (0x80 / 0xAC are the CADs' own losses).

Remaining in this ticket: the section 6 plumbing and E5.

* Section 6 plumbing and E5 (2026-10-06, clautolisp 2.2.204 / alfe 2.2.150)

An audit found most situations parsed and not acted on (only `source` reached
every backend; `file` and `log` were read nowhere; the bare -E did not reach
`source`; no warn-and-ignore existed although point 4 above said DONE).
Done now:
  - the bare -E / --encoding reaches `source` and `terminal` (the legacy
    load-encoding / io-encoding slots are recomputed through
    cli-situation-encoding; a specific option wins in any order);
  - an unknown -E<name> is an error listing the situations;
  - `terminal` in the clautolisp tool: the G1 code moved from alfe to the
    shared autolisp-cli (terminal.lisp); clautolisp applies it at start-up;
  - `file`: -Efile[-read|-write] -> *AUTOLISP-FILE-READ-ENCODING* /
    *AUTOLISP-FILE-WRITE-ENCODING*, consulted first by OPEN's default;
  - AutoCAD: -Econsole is warned about and ignored (console product-fixed);
    the bare -E no longer reaches the console / pipe codecs (a blanket
    -E UTF-8 read the UTF-16LE pipe as UTF-8); -Ecadstdio stays the explicit
    override (by E3 the drain carries the CAD's file bytes);
  - AutoCAD source staging: a -Esource code-page file is handed to AutoCAD
    in cp1252 without a BOM (right at LISPSYS 0 / 1 / 2 by E1) instead of
    UTF-8 + BOM (wrong at LISPSYS 0, which ignores the BOM);
  - --list-situations rows corrected.
E5 answered: -e is gone (rejected as an unknown option); -E / --encoding is the
every-situation shorthand; -Eterminal exists with -in / -out.
Still open: `log` (no reader of the CAD log exists yet), `console` -in and
the CAD-side `file` default (the bootstrap `open` shim's default encoding,
point 5), and one keyword per situation on start-engine (point 1).

* Section 6 points 1 and 5, console -in (2026-10-06, clautolisp 2.2.210 / alfe 2.2.152, MR !432)

- Point 1: start-engine takes one keyword per situation and direction
  (situation-engine-keywords); alfe --clautolisp in subprocess mode forwards
  -Efile-read / -Efile-write / -Eterminal-in / -Eterminal-out / -Elog.
- console -in: clautolisp -- the console IS the terminal (-Econsole folds
  into it unless -Eterminal is given); BricsCAD -- -Econsole-in warned and
  ignored, a bare -E no longer reaches the drain codec.
- Point 5, the CAD-side `file' default -- MEASURED first (probe-open-encoding:
  AutoCAD 2022 job 16967893842, BricsCAD V25 Windows 16968316289, V26 macOS
  16967893843), writing "A" e-acute "B":
    AutoCAD 2022 (LISPSYS 0): default cp1252; ANY third argument "too many
      arguments"; ,ccs= accepted and ignored; read decodes cp1252.
    BricsCAD V25 Windows: default cp1252; "utf8"/"utf8-bom" accepted, no
      effect; ,ccs=UTF-8 writes UTF-8 WITH a BOM, ,ccs=UTF-16LE UTF-16LE with a
      BOM; read never decodes (octets, BOM skipped).
    BricsCAD V26 macOS: always UTF-8 without a BOM; read returns octets.
  So the only honourable case is WRITE on BricsCAD Windows: alfe-open* in the
  bootstrap opens a plain "w"/"a" with ,ccs=UTF-8 / UTF-16LE, the loader
  rewriting OPEN only when that is active (*ALFE-OPEN-WRITE-CCS*); every other
  case is warned and ignored (cad-file-encoding-plan). clautolisp's own OPEN
  diverges from the vendors on the third argument:
  open-third-argument-dialect-divergence.
- Found on the way: alfe-load-user-setq-clobbers-loader-locals.

Still open here: `log' -- alfe reading the CAD's own log file (no reader
exists; clautolisp now WRITES per-drawing logs, MR !428); AutoCAD's "utf8"
third argument at LISPSYS 1 / 2 (unmeasured: the runner is at 0).
* The `log' situation (2026-10-06, clautolisp 2.2.209)

Measured (probe-logfile, MR !428): AutoCAD 2022 writes its LOGFILEMODE log in
windows-1252 (an e-acute PRINCed reads back as 233). BricsCAD V25 logs only the
command channel, so the PRINC marker never reached the file; its accented
prompts read back whole, which fits windows-1252 or UTF-8 with a BOM -- not
settled. clautolisp now writes per-drawing logs (cador command-log.lisp) and
-Elog reaches them (*AUTOLISP-LOG-ENCODING*): default windows-1252 under the
AutoCAD dialects, UTF-8 otherwise. Still open here: console -in, the CAD-side
file default, one keyword per situation on start-engine.

* The remainder (2026-10-07, clautolisp 2.2.231 / alfe 2.2.231)

The two items the 2026-10-06 sections left open, and what became of them.

** AutoCAD's "utf8" third argument at LISPSYS 1 / 2 -- MEASURED, then forwarded

It was measured meanwhile (E1, job 16980926802, MR !438; recorded in
autocad-open-encoding-lispsys-divergence): at LISPSYS 1 / 2 (open f "w" "utf8")
writes UTF-8 without a BOM, "utf8-bom" with one, and a plain "w" still writes
cp1252; a plain "r" decodes UTF-8 (BOM skipped) and falls back to cp1252, while
"r" "utf8" returns the BOM as 65279 and stops at a byte that is not UTF-8. At 0
any third argument is an error. clautolisp's OWN OPEN under the AutoCAD dialects
follows that in 2.2.222 (MR !450, autocad-open-encoding-lispsys-divergence) --
not touched here. Done here, on the alfe side:

- -Efile-write UTF-8 under --autocad is forwarded: CAD-FILE-ENCODING-PLAN
  returns the third argument "utf8" (a third value), emitted as
  *ALFE-OPEN-WRITE-ARG*; the bootstrap's alfe-open* passes it to a plain
  (open f "w") in alfe-loaded code when the CAD's LISPSYS is 1 or 2
  (alfe-lispsys-unicode-p, read in the CAD at the call: LISPSYS is fixed when
  AutoCAD starts and alfe cannot know it beforehand). At LISPSYS 0 it warns
  once on alfe's error channel and opens the plain mode. Other write encodings
  on AutoCAD are still warned about and not forwarded.
- NOT forwarded: an (open f "a") -- what AutoCAD does with "a" "utf8" /
  "utf8-bom" (a second BOM in the middle?) is unmeasured; warned once.
- Reads: the -Efile-read warning now states the measured read defaults (it is
  still not forwarded: the default read is the best there is at every level).
- G3, the deported loader (autolisp-source-open-encoded-try): on AutoCAD it
  passed "r" "utf8" for an explicit -Esource UTF-8. By the measurement above
  that is never better than a plain "r" (an error, so the fallback, at 0) and
  worse at 1 / 2 (a BOM read as a character, a truncated read on a cp1252
  byte), so AutoCAD now always gets the plain "r". Test
  protocol-autocad-source-open-takes-no-third-argument.

** The `log' situation -- alfe now reads the CAD's log: --cad-log FILE

The bootstrap already pointed LOGFILEPATH at the workdir's logs/ and set
LOGFILEMODE 1 (log / full phases); nothing read the file, and -Elog was
accepted and silently unused on the CAD backends. Now:

- --cad-log FILE (alfe option; cli-options slot cad-log in the shared struct):
  after the engine is down and before the workdir goes,
  ALFE.BACKEND:COLLECT-ENGINE-LOG (new generic; CAD backends ->
  CAD-COMMON:COLLECT-CAD-LOG) reads every file of logs/ except alfe's
  debug.log, oldest first, decodes it, normalises CR LF to LF, and alfe writes
  it to FILE in UTF-8 (one "==> NAME <==" line per file when there are several).
- Decoding: the explicit -Elog (the bare -E does not reach it -- the CAD does
  not change its log's encoding for alfe); else a BOM's encoding; else
  windows-1252 on MS-Windows -- measured for AutoCAD 2022, and right under BOTH
  open BricsCAD V25 hypotheses (windows-1252, or UTF-8 with a BOM) -- and the
  auto-detect cascade elsewhere (macOS: no log in batch, nothing measured).
- No log: a warning, no file. --clautolisp: a warning, no file (clautolisp
  writes its own log where LOGFILEPATH points; -Elog sets its encoding there).

** Probes written, results to read (nothing guessed)

1. BricsCAD V25 Windows log encoding (windows-1252, or UTF-8 with a BOM?):
   - probes/sources/probe-logfile.lsp gained the line "the log's (size chars
     lines high-chars first-high-codes)": the file's byte size against what
     (open "r") reads back. Single-octet file: size = chars + line ends; UTF-8:
     each accented character one byte more, plus 3 for a BOM. Runs in
     vendor:probes:* (family suite).
   - scripts/run-encoding-experiment.{ps1,sh} gained a LOG step: a native PRINC
     of e-acute + euro and a LINE command, run with --cad-log --keep-workdir,
     reports what --cad-log decoded (code points) AND the raw log bytes (size,
     first 16 bytes, the bytes around the first one above 7F). Runs in
     encoding:experiment:{bricscad:windows,autocad:windows,bricscad:macos}.
   TO MEASURE: whether the raw BricsCAD log starts with EF BB BF and how its
   first accented prompt character is encoded. If UTF-8 without a BOM (neither
   hypothesis), the default decode rule must change.
2. APPEND with an encoding (alfe already appends ,ccs= to "a" on BricsCAD
   Windows, unmeasured):
   - probe-open-encoding.lsp: "w,ccs=UTF-8" then "a,ccs=UTF-8", the same in
     UTF-16LE, "w,ccs=UTF-8" then a plain "a", and "w" "utf8" then "a" "utf8"
     -- sizes after the write and after the append (UTF-8+BOM 7 -> 9 = no
     second BOM, 12 = a second one; UTF-16LE 8 -> 10 / 12).
   - lispsys-bom-probe.lsp: "w"/"a" with "utf8" and "utf8-bom" at each LISPSYS
     level ("open-a-..." lines; 4 -> 6, 7 -> 9 or 12).
   TO MEASURE: if no second BOM, alfe-open* may forward "a" on AutoCAD too; if
   BricsCAD writes a second BOM on "a,ccs=", alfe must stop forwarding "a".
3. End-to-end confirmation of the AutoCAD forwarding:
   lispsys-forward-probe.lsp, run by run-lispsys-bom-probe.ps1 at each level
   with -Efile-write utf-8 ("ENC E1F" lines + WARN lines).
   TO MEASURE: LISPSYS 0 -> "w SIZE 3" + the LISPSYS warning; 1 / 2 ->
   "w SIZE 4"; every level "a SIZE 1" + the append warning.

