(encoding:experiment:lispsys-bom:autocad:windows). Read from the job traces
and artefacts, on bytes. (The Windows runner keeps dist/encoding/ between
jobs, so each Windows job's artefact also carries the other jobs' reports;
each answer below comes from the job that wrote it, checked against its own
trace.)

** 1. BricsCAD V25 log encoding -- SETTLED: windows-1252, no BOM

Job 16998925784, LOG step (run-encoding-experiment.ps1 -Backend bricscad):
#+begin_example
[LOG] decoded: 11 lines
[LOG] decoded line: D?part de la ligne : 0,0 | high code points: 233
[LOG] decoded line: D?finissez l'extr?mit? ou [Angle/Longueur/annUler] : 1,1 | high code points: 233 233 233
[LOG] raw Dessin1_2026-10-08_11-54-58.log: 475 bytes; first 16: 0D 0A 2D 2D 2D 2D 2D 2D 2D 2D 2D 2D 20 5B 20 42; first byte above 7F at offset 143: 0D 0A 44 E9 70 61 72 74
#+end_example
No EF BB BF at the start; e-acute is the single byte E9 ("\r\nDepart"). Neither
of the two open hypotheses was "UTF-8 without a BOM", so alfe's --cad-log
default (a BOM's encoding, else windows-1252 on MS-Windows) was already right
and is unchanged (comments, manuals and a regression test on these bytes,
cad-log-decodes-the-measured-bricscad-v25-log, updated in 2.3.9); the
--cad-log copy (cad-log-bricscad.txt, UTF-8) reads "Départ de la ligne",
"Définissez l'extrémité". As known, BricsCAD logs only the command channel: the
PRINC marker is absent. The probe-logfile size line (vendor:probes:*) was not
part of these jobs; the raw bytes above settle the question anyway.
- BricsCAD V26 macOS (job 16998925783): "the CAD wrote no log ... BricsCAD on
  macOS writes none in batch mode" -- confirmed again, nothing to decode.
- AutoCAD 2022 (job 16998925785): the LOG step gave NO evidence. The raw log
  =empty_129366860.log= is 210 bytes, all below 80 ("first byte above 7F at
  offset 210: none"); it holds the header, "nil", ._QSAVE and ._QUIT -- no
  LOG-MARKER, no LINE prompts -- and the trace has no "LOG WRITTEN" line, so
  the probe form failed in the CAD before its PRINC (the report's filter
  dropped the ERROR line; it keeps ERROR lines from 2.3.9 on). AutoCAD's log
  encoding stays as measured by probe-logfile (MR !428): windows-1252.
Consequence outside alfe: clautolisp writes its own log in UTF-8 under the
BricsCAD dialects -- filed as cador-bricscad-windows-log-encoding (the cador
sources are not touched here).

** 2. OPEN in APPEND mode with an encoding -- AutoCAD SETTLED, BricsCAD NOT MEASURED

AutoCAD 2022, job 16998925786 (lispsys-bom-probe.lsp: "A" e-acute "B" written
with (open f "w" ENC), one e-acute appended with (open f "a" ENC), sizes):
#+begin_example
[LISPSYS 0] ENC E1 open-a-utf8 ERR nombre d'arguments trop important
[LISPSYS 0] ENC E1 open-a-utf8-bom ERR nombre d'arguments trop important
[LISPSYS 1] ENC E1 open-a-utf8 SIZES (4 6)
[LISPSYS 1] ENC E1 open-a-utf8-bom SIZES (7 9)
[LISPSYS 2] ENC E1 open-a-utf8 SIZES (4 6)
[LISPSYS 2] ENC E1 open-a-utf8-bom SIZES (7 9)
#+end_example
So at LISPSYS 1 / 2 "a" "utf8" appends UTF-8 (2 bytes for e-acute) and
"a" "utf8-bom" adds NO second BOM (7 -> 9, not 12); at 0 the third argument is
an error, as on "w". Acted on (alfe 2.3.9): alfe-open* now passes
*ALFE-OPEN-WRITE-ARG* to a plain "a" as well as to "w" at LISPSYS 1 / 2 (at 0:
not forwarded, one warning per run for both); the "a ... is not given"
warning is gone. Test protocol-alfe-load-forwards-open-write-arg.

BricsCAD: probe-open-encoding.lsp (the "w,ccs=UTF-8" -> "a,ccs=UTF-8",
UTF-16LE, plain "a", "w" "utf8" -> "a" "utf8" cases) runs in the
vendor:probes:* jobs, and none of those was among the runs read here. The
BricsCAD ",ccs=" append is therefore STILL UNMEASURED, and alfe's forwarding
of ,ccs= on "a" for BricsCAD/Windows is KEPT unchanged until it is (if
BricsCAD writes a second BOM there, that forwarding must stop).

** 3. End-to-end AutoCAD -Efile-write forwarding -- NOT MEASURED (the probe's premise was wrong)

Job 16998925786, lispsys-forward-probe.lsp under -Efile-write utf-8:
#+begin_example
[LISPSYS 0 forward] ENC E1F LISPSYS 0 FORWARDED-ARG "utf8"
[LISPSYS 0 forward] ENC E1F w SIZE 3
[LISPSYS 0 forward] ENC E1F a SIZE 1
[LISPSYS 1 forward] ENC E1F LISPSYS 1 FORWARDED-ARG "utf8"
[LISPSYS 1 forward] ENC E1F w SIZE 3
[LISPSYS 1 forward] ENC E1F a SIZE 1
[LISPSYS 2 forward] ENC E1F LISPSYS 2 FORWARDED-ARG "utf8"
[LISPSYS 2 forward] ENC E1F w SIZE 3
[LISPSYS 2 forward] ENC E1F a SIZE 1
#+end_example
and no WARN line at any level (the report filter keeps lines matching WARN,
case-insensitively). Expected was "w SIZE 4" at LISPSYS 1 / 2 and a WARN at
every level. *ALFE-OPEN-WRITE-ARG* was set in the CAD, but alfe-open* was
never called: alfe sends the -l file as a (load ...) request, which the
bootstrap evaluates through autolisp-eval-request-form, and only alfe-load
rewrites OPEN. So the run measured the UNFORWARDED OPEN, not the forwarding;
the probe was wrong, and a real gap surfaced: -Efile-write never reaches the
-l file's own OPEN calls, silently -- filed as
alfe-efile-write-not-applied-to-l-file (pjb-decision: rewrite OPEN in the
request path, or warn). Done here: the manuals now say so;
run-lispsys-bom-probe.ps1 writes a one-line entry file
(alfe-load "<lispsys-forward-probe.lsp>") and gives THAT to -l, so the next run
measures the forwarding itself. New expectations (with "a" now forwarded):
LISPSYS 0 -> w 3, a 1, one WARN naming LISPSYS; LISPSYS 1 / 2 -> w 4, a 2, no
WARN.

** Still open here

- The BricsCAD/Windows append with ",ccs=" (probe-open-encoding, in a
  vendor:probes:bricscad:windows run): keep or stop the ,ccs= forwarding on
  "a".
- The end-to-end AutoCAD check, re-run with the corrected probe
  (encoding:experiment:lispsys-bom:autocad:windows).
Everything else of sections 4 to 6, and the `log' situation, is done.
